WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.189 --> 00:00:05.730
KATHERINE: The United States cannot kill its way out
of the war against Islamist terror.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.020
To turn the tide, America must rethink
its strategy.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.680
Since the attacks on New York and Washington
on September 11th, 2001, the United States

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.240
has defined its enemies as specific terror
groups.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:32.860
PRESIDENT BUSH: Our war on terror
begins with al Qaeda, but it does not end there.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:42.640
It will not end until every terrorist group
of global reach has been found, stopped, and defeated.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.500
KATHERINE: The Obama administration defined ISIS as enemy
number one, and its control of Mosul and Raqqa

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:51.510
as the pressing problem.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.642
PRESIDENT OBAMA: We’re going after ISIL
from their stronghold right

00:00:54.642 --> 00:01:00.699
in downtown Raqqa, to Libya, where we took
out Abu Nabil, the ISIL leader there.

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:03.229
The point is, ISIL leaders cannot hide.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:07.440
And our next message to them is simple:
&nbsp;You are next.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.400
KATHERINE: Trump doubled-down by killing even more ISIS members and driving them out of their captured

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.410
lands.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:18.240
PRESIDENT TRUMP: And we are effectively ridding
the world of ISIS.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.940
I see that as a primary role, and that’s
what we’re going to do, whether it’s in

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.630
Iraq or in Libya or anywhere else.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.500
KATHERINE: But while America’s current anti-ISIS campaign may severely degrade them, it will not defeat

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.110
or destroy them.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.490
Instead, it may help al Qaeda assume the lead
in Syria and facilitate the rise of the next

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.630
ISIS incarnation in Iraq.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.090
In short, the cardinal error of American foreign

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:50.430
policy since 9/11 has been the pursuit of
specific, named terror groups as the enemy.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.750
The US needs to rethink that strategy.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.240
By focusing U.S. military, legal and policy
tools on the destruction of particular groups

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.870
like ISIS and al Qaeda, policymakers miss
the real enemy: the Salafi-jihadi movement.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:08.420
To understand the way forward, we need to
understand this movement.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.980
Let’s be clear: The root cause of Islamist
terror is not specific terror groups, like

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.780
ISIS and al Qaeda.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.720
The cause of terrorism isn’t the religion
of Islam, either.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.200
It’s not Muslims.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.810
The roots of Islamist terror sprout from Salafi-jihadism,
a fringe sect with extremist beliefs about

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.660
the practice of Islam.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:38.120
The practice and observance of Sunni Islam,
like all major contemporary religions, varies greatly.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.120
On one end of the spectrum are Sunni who are
fundamentally secular.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.959
There are also devout mainstream Sunni.

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:51.150
Salafism is a trend in Sunni Islam, and among
Salafis, the spectrum of belief ranges from

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.570
those who abstain from politics and violence,
to those who pursue political power to effect

00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:00.760
change, to those who justify violence for
political purposes.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.880
Positioned at the extreme are those who seek
to destroy the state and establish a new polity

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.680
under sharia, though even these groups disagree
among themselves over when, where, and against

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.280
whom violent acts are justified to achieve
their aims.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.160
Salafi-jihadi ideology brings together groups
and individuals into a network that seeks

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:25.040
the same shared outcome, and these groups
and individuals comprise the Salafi-jihadi base.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.560
It’s these Salafi-jihadi groups that pose
the threat of terrorism, Al Qaeda and ISIS

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.099
most prominent among them.

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:34.930
Yet the majority of Muslims reject the end

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:39.860
state that al Qaeda, ISIS, and the broader
Salafi-jihadi base pursue.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:45.440
So how has Salafi-jihadism – a once-isolated
ideology – become so powerful?

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:51.700
In short, vulnerable Sunni Muslim populations
have turned to Salafi-jihadi groups for help.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.720
Sunni populations across the Muslim world
are now under threat – both real and perceived –

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:05.470
from Iran, Shi’a Muslims, the Assad
regime, Russia, the Kurds, and even the West,

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:06.470
among others.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.569
They are looking for help to survive.

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:13.680
Modern Salafi-jihadism has been present in
the Muslim world for decades,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.340
but at the edges of society.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:22.240
In stable countries, Sunni populations had
no reason to tolerate extremism in large numbers.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.800
But popular grievances, beginning with the
Arab Spring in 2011, inspired uprisings that

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.180
then spread and spiraled out of control, disrupting
the stability of the Middle East.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:36.270
There are now at least six failed states in
the Muslim world.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:42.560
Weak states from Nigeria to Egypt to Afghanistan
are also unable to deliver basic goods to

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.970
their populations.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:51.880
In these destabilized countries, the Salafi-jihadi
base delivers protection, stability, and assistance

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.150
to these threatened and aggrieved populations.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:05:00.460
Sunni communities are now making short-term
decisions to tolerate the Salafi-jihadi base,

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.000
ending the base’s decades in isolation.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.020
So, if ISIS and al Qaeda are taking advantage

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:10.370
of weak Sunni populations, why can’t the
United States just target the leaders of these

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.479
Salafi-jihadi groups and help partners recapture
terrain?

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:17.870
While al-Qaeda and ISIS are the most dangerous
and prominent groups in the Salafi-jihadi

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:23.830
movement, attacking them does nothing to weaken the Salafi-jihadi movement itself, and doesn’t actually

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.870
break the relationships that Salafi-jihadi
groups have with vulnerable Sunni populations.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:34.400
US policy decisions, particularly those regarding
Syria, have fed a ground truth narrative that

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.150
the US doesn’t care about the Sunni or,
worse, is actively supporting the aggressors

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.150
against the Sunni.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:45.980
In fact, the United States has implicitly allied with
all actors fighting against ISIS in Syria

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.320
except for the majority-Sunni opposition group.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.260
The US even has a de facto partnership against
ISIS with the Iranian- and Russian-backed

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:56.740
Assad regime, which was compounded by President Trump’s decision

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.420
to stop arming the Sunni opposition group.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:03.660
The US remains focused on disrupting or destroying
the cells of al Qaeda and ISIS that are plotting

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.240
major operations against the West, reducing
the military strength of select groups – especially

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.220
through regaining territory – and killing
group leaders.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:18.340
This does nothing but change which group vulnerable
Sunnis run to, or which leaders drive the

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.140
Salafi-jihadi base.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:24.720
Al Qaeda and ISIS each seek to be the leaders of the Salafi-jihadi movement.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:30.860
Imagine that these filings represent the individuals
and groups that believe in the Salafi-jihadi ideology.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:36.260
Al Qaeda was the only global organization,
and like a magnet draws the filings to it,

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.680
so too, did al Qaeda assume the leadership
position.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.150
The rise of ISIS added another pole to the
movement.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.430
Al Qaeda’s and ISIS’s competition didn’t
weaken the movement.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.400
Both advance it.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.860
Defeating ISIS will position al Qaeda to capture
the remnants of that organization.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.500
Defeating al Qaeda, too, weakens the movement,
but another organization will rise in its place.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:04.120
While it is possible – and may even be necessary
– to target individuals or networks, guns

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.080
and bombs cannot destroy a movement.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:12.180
The elements of American power now operate
against a mere fraction of the movement.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:17.070
They may destroy that fraction, but will not
destroy or even defeat the movement itself.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:23.560
This is why the US must look beyond the groups
and focus on the Salafi-jihadi base itself.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:26.300
So, can we win?

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.560
Knowing what we know now, what should we do
to move forward?

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:34.860
As a first step, the United States must work
to resolve local wars and conflicts that seemingly

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:41.039
fall outside of American interests, but actually
strengthen the Salafi-jihadi base.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.260
None of these wars have a military solution.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:47.160
Resolving them will be no easy task.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.180
But an end to the conflicts would help to
demobilize Sunnis, and would constrain the

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:57.120
Salafi-jihadi base’s ability to insinuate
itself into Sunni populations and generate support.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:04.800
Additionally, in areas penetrated by or vulnerable
to the Salafai-jihadi base, US policies must

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:11.580
promote accountable governments that are legitimate, transparent, and responsive to the needs of citizens.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.660
The only path to victory is resolving local
conflicts and societal upheaval, focusing

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:22.860
on the Sunni population, and breaking the
ties between the people and the Salafi-jihadi base.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:28.980
Anything less ensures another generation of
Americans will be fighting the same war and losing.

